Spring Security — the mental model
Spring Security is a framework that wraps your app in a chain of filters. Every HTTP request passes through them in order. Each filter can:
Look at the request
Modify it
Reject it (send 401/403)
Pass it to the next filter
By default, Spring Security blocks everything. If you add the dependency and don't configure anything, all your endpoints will return 401 Unauthorized.


1. Browser/Postman sends:
   GET /employees/allEmployees
   Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJKb2huIn0.xyz

2. Tomcat receives the request → passes it into the Spring Security filter chain.

3. Our JwtFilter runs (first, because we inserted it early):
   a. Reads "Authorization" header → "Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJKb2huIn0.xyz"
   b. Strips "Bearer " → "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJKb2huIn0.xyz"
   c. jwtUtil.validateToken(...) → recomputes signature → matches → true
   d. jwtUtil.extractUsername(...) → "John"
   e. SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth) → marks "John is authenticated"
   f. chain.doFilter(request, response) → pass along

4. Spring's remaining filters run. They see SecurityContextHolder has an authenticated user.
   → the URL rule `.anyRequest().authenticated()` is satisfied → request proceeds.

5. DispatcherServlet routes to EmployeeController.getAllEmpController().

6. Controller → Service → Repo → DB → returns the list.

7. Response 200 OK with JSON goes back to the client.